Native 120 FPS steps down instead of running slow; the pause menu's POST FX
switch works on the paused frame; texture dedither also under bilinear.

120 FPS in slow motion. Reported as "slow motion, even though the FPS is
100+". The session's heartbeat ring confirms it: 120 Hz VBlank (282k cycles
a field) at 68-96 VBlanks a wall second - the whole game, music included, at
0.57-0.80 of its speed - and 120 never let go. The host check fired, but under
the launcher's forced gate ("Prefer 60") the branch that handles it ran on
every loop, not only on the loop that completes a window, and cleared the
host streak each time; the streak never reached two. Now:
  - a PENDING verdict (no window completed) returns before any branch, so
    streaks count windows again, forced gate or not;
  - at 120 one slow window steps down (C2_120_HOST_STREAK 1): the cost of
    missing 120 is slow motion, not dropped frames;
  - a host step-down backs off on its own schedule, 10 s doubling to 160 s,
    which a load does not reset (a new scene does not make the machine
    faster); ten comfortable seconds at 120 do. Scene (hold) step-downs keep
    their rules. native_120fps_host_fallbacks reports the host ones.
Modelled in tuning/native120_sim with a host that manages ~91 fields a second:
1.0 s of slow motion at 0.76x, then 60 at full speed on the same host, a
retry after 10 s that steps down at once, the next after 20 s.

The other half of that session's slowness was not in this tree:
autocompile_degraded - "overlay autocompile failed 3 consecutive runs (last
exit 1)". Choosing Direct3D 12 makes the launcher write renderer = "d3d12"
into game.toml, and the recompiler binary compile_overlays.py runs predated
patch 0041, so its --overlay-config-hash rejected the file. Every session on
Direct3D 12 ran uncompiled code interpreted - including the VSync page the
60/120 mode patches, ~1,950 interpreted dispatches a VBlank against ~240 on
24 Sep. _build/build_recompiler.ps1 rebuilds it from this tree (codegen hash
still 3d2b5a19, same cache).

POST FX in the pause menu looked dead. The pause loop redraws the window from
the drawable captured at the last live present (hold-last). The switch called
gl_renderer_invalidate_present(), which drops that capture - so from then on
the pause loop presented nothing: the menu stopped updating until it was
closed, and neither the new value nor the picture changed. Now the switch
only flips the setting, and:
  - with post-processing configured the image always goes through the
    chain's input target, even switched off, where glpfx_end copies it
    through unchanged (neutral settings);
  - the hold-last redraw re-runs the chain from that input - the image as it
    was before any effect - over the rect it last covered, with whatever is
    switched on now. So the paused frame shows the switch at once. The
    texture dedither is part of drawing the scene and follows on resume.

Texture dedither applied only to nearest sampling; with bilinear texture
filtering (the launcher default) it did nothing. Each bilinear tap is now
smoothed as the nearest texel is (transparent taps skipped). TEX_FS and its
HLSL twin changed together; gpu_gl12_shaders.h regenerated.

Checked with tuning/renderer_parity (new PARITY_FILTER=1 and a paused-toggle
step): VRAM bit-identical GL vs Direct3D 12 with bilinear + dedither; the
paused frame with POST FX off differs in every pixel from on (a visible
grade), and on again matches it to the 1/255 output dither; no post-FX and
SMAA/CAS runs byte-identical across APIs.

No file here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake; overlay shards are unaffected.

